__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
logs/
results/
analysis/
assets/*
.hf_assets_cache/
_test_tmp/
run_progress.txt
# The meter reading that tools/usage_delta.py --before records.  Per-machine, per-key
# state that would be wrong in a clone rather than reproducible in one.
usage_baseline.json

# Model logs and episode records retrieved from the lab machine.  The two
# archives and everything derived from them ARE committed, because a clone has to
# be able to re-derive every number in the report without the lab machine: the
# 660-episode v7 sweep exists nowhere else, and the v5 archive is byte-identical to
# the loose tree on the development machine.  What is NOT committed is a redundant
# unpacked copy and a redundant subset archive; both were checked key-by-key and
# byte-by-byte (SHA-256) against the committed archives first.  See the README,
# "Reproducing this repository".
lab_logs/extracted/
lab_logs/bao_v7_qwen.tgz

# Scratch files used to pass a multi-line commit message to `git commit -F`.
# They are created and deleted around the commit, but one was committed twice by
# accident, so they are ignored rather than trusted to be cleaned up in time.
COMMIT_MSG_*.txt

# Local scratch for examining rendered frames: crop/zoom scripts and the crops they
# produce.  Committed once by accident, and one of the files in it was a check whose
# criterion was later found to be wrong -- a misleading tool in the repo is worse than
# no tool, so these live outside version control unless one is promoted deliberately.
render_check/

# Throwaway probes that ask the workstation what an API actually offers.  They are run
# once, read once, and then either deleted or promoted into tools/ deliberately.  See
# MANUAL section 8.3 rule 6: a diagnostic is not a deliverable.
diag_pxr.py

# Output of the sandbox file-permission diagnostic (see MANUAL section 8.3).  Its report and
# backup files are evidence for one investigation, not project content.
.acl-report/

# Word's lock file for an open document: it appears whenever a .docx is open and
# vanishes when it closes, so committing it would record a transient state as content.
~$*.docx

# CREDENTIALS.  The remote is HTTPS, so a personal access token can end up in a file, an environment
# dump or a shell history captured into a log, and this repository is public.  Nothing here was ever
# committed, but the patterns were absent, which means one careless `git add -A` was all it took.
# A token that reaches a public history has to be revoked, not rewritten.
.git-credentials
.netrc
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*_token.txt
*token*.json
.env
.env.*

# Stage 1 scene extension: the Isaac material sets the scenes need, vendored so that a clone
# has them.  The lines above ignore assets/ wholesale; these are the exceptions, and they are
# deliberately narrow: only the material sets, downscaled by tools/shrink_textures.py -- the
# raw download is 473 MB of 4K maps for a 512 px render.
!assets/isaac/
assets/isaac/**/.thumbs/

# Everything vendored from OUTSIDE Assets/Isaac: the vegetation and NVIDIA's outdoor environment,
# which the park needs because assets/isaac contains no plant of any kind.  This exception is easy
# to forget and its symptom is silent -- the files sit on the machine that fetched them, the
# catalogue references them, and every render on a fresh clone draws the fallback box instead.  It
# was in fact forgotten: the park's eleven props came back as "0 from assets" on the lab machine
# while measuring perfectly well here.  test_bao_assets checks that a referenced asset is vendored,
# and that check reads the working tree, so it cannot catch a file git was told to ignore.
!assets/bucket/
assets/bucket/**/.thumbs/

# =============================================================================================
# THE LAB MACHINE'S WORKING TREE.  Added 2026-10-11 after `git status --short` there printed more
# than eighty untracked entries: render logs, one-off probe scripts, diagnostic reports and
# directories of old previews, accumulated across weeks of work.
#
# The reason this matters is not tidiness.  The working instructions for that machine say to finish
# each round with `git add -A`, and on a tree in that state `git add -A` would sweep all of it into
# one commit on a PUBLIC repository.  That is how a stray diagnostic, a local path or a credential
# ends up in a history that cannot be rewritten away.
#
# These patterns are deliberately narrow -- only extensions and names with no plausible project
# content -- because a broad `*.txt` or `*.py` would silently exclude real work instead.  Anything
# that IS content must be named explicitly in the commit rules, not rely on an ignore pattern.
#
# The already-tracked exceptions this must not swallow: requirements.txt, lab_logs/summary.txt,
# lab_logs/*.csv.  Verified before adding.
# =============================================================================================
*.log
*.log.[0-9]*
*.pid
*_report.txt
*_measurements.txt
diag_*.py
bao_probe*.py
git_head_*.txt
summary_v*.txt
# Directories of old previews and previous runs.  Each held a handful of PNGs produced by a camera
# setting that has since changed; they are evidence for a decision already made, not content.
preview/
preview_*/
prev/
prev2/
prev_*/
scene_preview/
views/
all_stage1.*/
fix_stage1.*/
p_l0/
p_m/
p_norobot/
park4/
park_check/
shelf_check/
look_down_check/
probe_emit/
probe_gain0/
probe_gain4/
_old_*/
_bad_quota/
pilot.log*
smoke.log*
sweep*.log
cap*.log
c[0-9].log
ds_*.log
prev_narrow*
sixlvl.log
run_*.log
professor_verify.log
watch.log
diagnose.log
lab_measure_*.log
lab_logs_before_pull/
fix_stage1.*.log

# The versioned run directories (v74, v76, v77, ...) were being ignored one at a time as each was
# noticed, which meant the newest one was always the one that got committed by accident.  A range is
# the right shape: they are all directories of old previews.
v[0-9][0-9]/

# A DERIVED file, ignored for the same reason logs/ and results/ are.  tools/parse_agent_logs.py
# writes it from the raw model I/O under logs/ with `--csv logs_reasoning.csv`, and logs/ is itself
# ignored, so committing the CSV would commit an artefact whose input is not in the repository --
# reproducible from nothing, and stale the moment the logs change.  The manual names it as an EXAMPLE
# of the tool's output, not as content.
logs_reasoning.csv



# Derived diagnostics from the dressing/lighting investigation: rendered frames, per-run logs and
# the stage_tree/report dumps.  Kept on disk for reading, never committed -- the numbers they
# support are quoted in STAGE1_DRESSING_FINDINGS.md, which is the thing that belongs in the repo.
diag_logs/

# One-off diagnostics (see the left/right proof in the HEADING_RIGHT comment)
_lr_test.py

# Runtime artefacts: stale dispatcher pids and the model-verification dump.  Both are produced by
# a run and mean nothing outside it; pid_* in particular accumulates one file per launch.
pid_*
model_verification.json

# Audit scratch from a review session: logs and per-module reports, not project content.
_audit/
_backup/

# Local/paper exports and archived legacy Stage-2 code are not source.
exports/
_archive/
